Caching is a critical Spark optimization technique.
It improves performance when the same dataset is reused multiple times in a pipeline.
Without caching → Spark recomputes the entire DAG repeatedly.
Understanding when and how to cache is a very common interview topic.

#CoreConcept
Spark follows lazy evaluation.
When you run transformations:

df.filter().groupBy().join()

Spark does not execute immediately.
Execution happens only when an action is triggered:
show()
count()
write()
collect()
If the same DataFrame is used again, Spark recomputes it from the source unless cached.


#Why Caching Is Needed
Consider this pipeline:
Read large dataset
Clean data

Use it for:
Aggregations
Joins
ML training
Data quality checks

#Withoutcaching:
Spark recomputes step 1 & 2 every time.
Caching stores intermediate data in memory (or disk).


#WithoutCaching (Recomputation)
Pipeline example:

Raw → Filter → Clean → Join → Aggregate

If reused:

Raw → Filter → Clean → Join
Raw → Filter → Clean → Aggregate

Spark re-runs Raw → Filter → Clean again.


#WithCaching
Pipeline becomes:

Raw → Filter → Clean → CACHE
                        ↓
                    Join
                    Aggregate

Spark reuses cached dataset.

Types of Persistence Levels
Spark supports multiple storage levels.

1.MEMORY_ONLY (Default)

df.cache()

Data stored only in memory.
Fastest but may cause recomputation if memory insufficient.

2. MEMORY_AND_DISK

df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)

If memory full → spill to disk.
Safer for large datasets.

3. DISK_ONLY
Stores only on disk.
Rarely used but useful when memory limited.

4. MEMORY_ONLY_SER
Stores serialized objects.
Uses less memory but slightly slower.


